---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `How Inflatable SUPs Are Made — Manufacturing Process Deep Dive | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Complete walkthrough of inflatable SUP manufacturing: PVC cutting, drop-stitch core assembly, fusion lamination, valve installation, EVA pad application, and quality testing. Factory tour from raw materials to finished board.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How Inflatable SUPs Are Made — Manufacturing Process Deep Dive',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Manufacturing Process</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Manufacturing</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">10 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">How Inflatable SUPs Are Made</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      From raw PVC rolls to a finished inflatable SUP board — every step of manufacturing directly affects rigidity,
      durability, and longevity. This guide covers the full production process.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>1. PVC Material Preparation</h2>
      <p>
        Manufacturing begins with military-grade PVC fabric in roll form. Typical inflatable SUP PVC is 0.9mm–1.2mm
        thick with a denier rating of 1100–1600 for the inner fabric layer. The PVC undergoes UV stabilizer treatment
        and is color-matched to design specifications. Computer-controlled cutting machines (CNC) precisely cut the top
        and bottom panels, rail strips, and reinforcement patches from the PVC rolls.
      </p>
      <h2>2. Drop-Stitch Core Assembly</h2>
      <p>
        The drop-stitch core is the defining technology of inflatable SUPs. Thousands of polyester threads are woven
        between two layers of PVC film. Each thread is approximately 6" long (matching the final board thickness). The
        threads stand perpendicular when the board is inflated, connecting the deck and hull. Higher thread density
        (measured in picks per inch) correlates with better rigidity. Afarer boards use 9-11 picks per inch density.
      </p>
      <h2>3. Fusion Lamination</h2>
      <p>
        The drop-stitch core is sandwiched between the top and bottom PVC panels. Fusion lamination uses high-frequency
        welding (also called RF welding or dielectric welding) to bond the panels to the core without adhesive. The
        fusion process melts the PVC layers together at the molecular level, creating a bond that is stronger than the
        surrounding material. Temperature, pressure, and welding time are precisely controlled.
      </p>
      <h2>4. Rail and Seam Welding</h2>
      <p>
        The rail strip (a separate piece of PVC that forms the board's side edge) is welded around the perimeter. This
        is the most critical step — rail seam failure is the most common cause of inflatable SUP failure. Quality
        manufacturers use overlapping seam construction rather than butt seams. Afarer uses triple-layer rail welding
        for impact resistance.
      </p>
      <h2>5. Valve Installation</h2>
      <p>
        Halkey-Roberts style valves are installed into a reinforced PVC patch on the deck. The valve base is
        fusion-welded, not glued. A secondary internal reinforcement plate distributes inflation pressure. Each valve
        undergoes individual air-leak testing at 20 PSI — above the board's rated maximum.
      </p>
      <h2>6. EVA Traction Pad Application</h2>
      <p>
        EVA foam deck pads are die-cut to shape and applied using industrial-grade contact adhesive or mechanical heat
        bonding. Multi-color EVA block piecing involves precisely fitting different colored EVA shapes together like a
        puzzle before bonding to the board. Laser-engraved patterns are created by etching the top EVA layer to reveal a
        contrasting bottom layer.
      </p>
      <h2>7. Accessory Attachment</h2>
      <p>
        D-rings for bungee cords, leash attachment points, and carry handles are attached using reinforced PVC patches
        that are fusion-welded to the hull. The center carry handle webbing is anchored through the EVA pad directly to
        the core PVC substrate, ensuring lifting loads are borne by the rigid hull structure.
      </p>
      <h2>8. Quality Testing</h2>
      <p>
        Every board is inflated to 15-18 PSI and held for 24 hours to verify pressure retention. Visual inspection
        checks seam alignment, EVA pad adhesion, and artwork registration. Random samples undergo destructive testing —
        peel tests on seams, tensile tests on drop-stitch threads, and UV exposure tests on printed graphics.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
